\\ This file is an example for pila language.

\*
Pila is an interpreted language based on piles.
And this, is how to write pila !
*\

18;                    \\ Add data on the main pile (named Pila)
45 67 98 90;           \\ Add more data on Pila

5 5 +;                 \\ Make 5 + 5 and place the result on Pila. CONSUME DATAS ON PILA !
7 7 .+;                \\ Make 7 + 7 and place the result on Pila. Doesn't consume datas on Pila

5 5 *;                 \\ Make 5 * 5 and place the result on Pila. CONSUME DATAS ON PILA !
5 5 .*;                \\ Make 5 * 5 and place the result on Pila. Doesn't consume datas on Pila

\*
To proceed arithmetic instructions, it's always the same method.
OPERAND_1 OPERAND_2  OPERATOR      consume operands on Pila
    and
OPERAND_1 OPERAND_2 .OPERATOR      doesn't consume operands on Pila

ARITHMETICS OPERATOR LIST :
 -
 +
 *
 /
 //
 %
*\

x = 5;                 \\ variable definition

x 3 +;                 \\ Add 3 to 5 (x = 5) and place the result on Pila. It make a copy of x value and place it on Pila,
                       \\ place the 3 on Pila and proceed the operation.

.x 60;                 \\ Redefine x value to 60. 60 it's consumed on Pila

.x 3 +;                \\ Add 3 to 5 (x = 5) and set the result to the variable x.
                       \\ It make a copy of x value and place it on Pila,
                       \\ place the 3 on Pila and proceed the operation.


